{"questions":[{"prompt":"A friend shows you a Monstera deliciosa cutting rooting in water. It has several roots that are firm and creamy white, each about 3 inches long, and a new leaf is beginning to unfurl. She asks whether it's ready to move to a pot with soil. What should you advise?","explanation":"According to the source material, readiness to pot a Monstera cutting requires both healthy roots and active growth. Healthy roots are described as firm and creamy white, and active growth means new leaves or shoots are developing. This cutting meets both criteria: it has firm, white roots and a new leaf unfurling. Therefore it is ready to be moved to a longer-term pot with well-draining medium.","expectedAnswer":"Pot it now — firm white roots plus active new growth indicate it is ready for a longer-term container","choices":["Pot it now — firm white roots plus active new growth indicate it is ready for a longer-term container","Keep it in water longer — roots need to turn brown before they can adapt to soil","Add rooting hormone to the water first, since it is required before transferring to soil","Wait until the new leaf fully hardens before potting, since any active growth makes transplant risky"],"topic":"root condition and growth stage to propagation and transplant decisions","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice","objective":"Evaluate root condition and growth stage to decide when a cutting is ready for transplant"},{"prompt":"You have a Monstera deliciosa with a long vine and a detached African violet leaf with its petiole. A beginner asks which tissue from each plant is appropriate for propagation. Based on species and available tissue, what is the best guidance?","explanation":"The sources specify that Monstera requires a stem piece with a node and axillary bud — a detached leaf or petiole alone cannot develop into a new plant. In contrast, African violets are listed as suited to leaf propagation. So the correct pairing is: use a stem section that includes a node for the Monstera, and use the leaf cutting for the African violet. Each species' structure determines which tissue type works.","expectedAnswer":"Use a stem section with a node for the Monstera; use the leaf cutting for the African violet","choices":["Use a stem section with a node for the Monstera; use the leaf cutting for the African violet","Use a leaf cutting for both, since any detached leaf can root and grow a new plant","Use a stem cutting for both, since leaf cuttings never produce new plants in any species","Root the Monstera leaf with its petiole in water; divide the African violet at the crown instead"],"topic":"Relate species, available stem or leaf tissue to propagation decisions","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice","objective":"Match propagation method to species based on available plant tissue"}]}
