{"questions":[{"prompt":"Read the passage: \"Maria bought a laptop last week. Yesterday she noticed the screen had a crack in it.\" Why does the writer use 'the screen' rather than 'a screen' in the second sentence?","explanation":"'The screen' is used because the laptop introduced in the first sentence establishes shared knowledge between writer and reader. Once a laptop is mentioned, its screen becomes identifiable through association—the reader can recover which screen is meant. This is an example of contextual recoverability: 'the' signals that the referent is already identifiable, even without a prior explicit mention of 'screen,' because it is a predictable part of the previously introduced laptop.","expectedAnswer":"The laptop in the first sentence makes its screen identifiable, so 'the' marks a referent recoverable from context.","choices":["The laptop in the first sentence makes its screen identifiable, so 'the' marks a referent recoverable from context.","'Screen' is uncountable, so it always requires 'the' rather than 'a.'","'The' is used because 'screen' has already been directly mentioned earlier in the passage.","'A screen' would mean Maria owns more than one laptop, so 'the' prevents confusion."],"topic":"Use shared reference","skillID":"","objectiveID":"","objective":"Explain how contextual recoverability determines the choice of 'the' for associated referents","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"},{"prompt":"Consider: \"Dogs are wonderful companions.\" vs. \"The dogs next door bark all night.\" Which explanation best accounts for the article difference between these two sentences?","explanation":"In 'Dogs are wonderful companions,' the bare plural refers to dogs as a general class—no particular dogs are intended. In 'The dogs next door bark all night,' the phrase 'next door' identifies a specific set of dogs recoverable by the reader, so 'the' is required. The key distinction is whether the noun phrase picks out a particular, identifiable group (requiring 'the') or refers to the class in general (bare plural, no article).","expectedAnswer":"The bare plural makes a general statement about the class; 'the' in the second picks out a particular identifiable group.","choices":["The bare plural makes a general statement about the class; 'the' in the second picks out a particular identifiable group.","'Dogs' in the first sentence is uncountable, so no article is needed; the second sentence corrects this.","Both sentences are generic, but 'the' is added in the second only because a location phrase follows the noun.","'A' cannot appear before plural nouns, so the writer had to drop the article in the first and use 'the' in the second."],"topic":"specific versus general meaning and countability to interpret article choices ac","skillID":"","objectiveID":"","objective":"Distinguish general-class bare plurals from particular-reference uses of 'the' with plural nouns","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"}]}
