istance, meaning contours are tightly packed—indicating steep terrain. The eastward segment also crosses 5 lines (another 200 ft gain) over 4 cm, meaning contours are more widely spaced—indicating gentler terrain. Both segments gain the same elevation (200 ft each), but the northward segment is much steeper because the same elevation change occurs over a shorter horizontal map distance.","expectedAnswer":"Both segments gain 200 ft, but the northward segment is much steeper than the eastward segment","choices":["Both segments gain 200 ft, but the northward segment is much steeper than the eastward segment","The northward segment gains more elevation because its contours are closer together","The eastward segment is steeper because it covers more map distance","The northward segment gains 200 ft and the eastward gains 40 ft"],"topic":"interval and spacing to compare terrain profiles and distinguish elevation from steepness","skillID":"","objectiveID":"","objective":"Use contour interval and map spacing to compare slope steepness along different trail segments","difficulty":3,"format":"Multiple Choice"}]}
